
EXPERIMENT 3: CREATE A CI PIPELINE USING GITHUB ACTIONS

STEP 1: CREATE PROJECT FOLDER

mkdir ASDD-Experiment3
cd ASDD-Experiment3
git init

STEP 2: CONFIGURE GIT (if not already configured)

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

STEP 3: CREATE A SAMPLE APPLICATION

notepad index.html

Enter the following HTML code and save the file:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>ASDD CI Pipeline</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello, ASDD!</h1>
    <p>My first CI pipeline project.</p>
</body>
</html>

STEP 4: CREATE THE WORKFLOW FOLDER

mkdir .github
mkdir .github\workflows

STEP 5: CREATE THE CI WORKFLOW FILE

notepad .github\workflows\ci.yml

Enter the following YAML code and save the file:

name: ASDD CI Pipeline

on:
  push:
  pull_request:

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Verify application
        run: test -f index.html

      - name: Display success message
        run: echo "CI Pipeline executed successfully!"

STEP 6: ADD AND COMMIT FILES

git add .
git commit -m "Create CI pipeline"

STEP 7: CREATE A GITHUB REPOSITORY

1. Open https://github.com/
2. Sign in to your GitHub account.
3. Click New repository.
4. Enter repository name: ASDD-Experiment3
5. Choose Public or Private.
6. Leave Add a README file unchecked.
7. Click Create repository.

STEP 8: CONNECT LOCAL REPOSITORY TO GITHUB

Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your actual GitHub username.

git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/ASDD-Experiment3.git

STEP 9: PUSH CODE TO GITHUB

git push -u origin main

STEP 10: VERIFY THE CI PIPELINE

1. Open your GitHub repository.
2. Click the Actions tab.
3. Open ASDD CI Pipeline.
4. Check that the workflow completed successfully.
5. Confirm that a green tick appears.
